        <div id="heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Heat Output, Honestly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every plug-in electric fireplace has the same heater: 1,500 watts on a 120-volt household circuit. One watt is about 3.412 BTU per hour, so 1,500 watts is roughly 5,100 BTU, which is why so many listings show 5,000, 5,118 or 5,200 BTU. That is the same output as a typical portable space heater, and it is genuinely useful for taking the chill off a room, but it is not enough to heat a large, poorly insulated space on a cold night.
          </p>

          <p>
            Room-size claims are where the marketing drifts. A rating of 400 square feet is a common and reasonable figure for supplemental heat in an average room. Claims of 1,000 square feet for the same 1,500 watts assume a well-insulated space, a mild outdoor temperature and a heater running constantly, so treat them as a best case. If you need to heat a whole floor, an electric fireplace is the wrong tool, and a heat pump or central system will do the job far more cheaply. If cooling is the bigger problem in your climate, our <a href="/best-evaporative-cooler/">best evaporative cooler</a> guide covers the low-cost option for dry regions.
          </p>

          <p>
            The way to get more heat from an electric fireplace is a hardwired 240-volt model. Built-in fireboxes such as the Dimplex Revillusion are listed at up to about 8,800 BTU when wired at 240 volts, compared with roughly 5,100 BTU for a 120-volt plug-in. Some three-sided units, such as the Amantii Tru-View, also advertise extra heat at 240 volts without publishing the wattage. If heat is the main reason you are buying, this is the specification to compare.
          </p>

          <p>
            All electric fireplaces convert essentially all of the electricity they use into heat in the room, because there is no flue losing warm air. That is often described as 100 percent efficient, which is true at the appliance but says nothing about cost: resistance heat is still one of the more expensive ways to heat a home per unit of warmth, which we cover in the running-cost section below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wiring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Wiring and Circuits
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 1,500-watt fireplace on a 120-volt circuit draws about 12.5 amps. That is within the limit of a standard 15-amp household circuit, but it uses most of it, which is why manufacturers typically recommend a dedicated circuit, or at least one without other large loads. If the outlet you plan to use also runs a vacuum, a hair dryer or another heater, expect tripped breakers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid extension cords and power strips for any electric fireplace heater unless the manual specifically allows them. Most manuals say to plug directly into a wall outlet, because an undersized cord carrying 12.5 amps for hours can overheat. If the cord will not reach, the right fix is a new outlet installed by an electrician, which on a recessed unit is usually part of the install anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hardwired 240-volt units need a dedicated circuit, appropriate wire and a breaker sized to the manual's requirements. In most jurisdictions that is permitted electrical work, and it is a job for a licensed electrician rather than a homeowner. Budget for it when comparing prices, because the cost of running a new 240-volt circuit can be a meaningful share of the total on a built-in project.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you also have a whole-house surge protector, or you are thinking about one, an electric fireplace's control board and LED flame effects are the kind of electronics it protects. Our guide to the <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">best whole-house surge protector</a> covers what to look for. If you are planning to run the fireplace from backup power during an outage, note that a 1,500-watt heater is a large load for a portable power station, a point we explain in our guide to <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-space-heater/">whether a portable power station can run a space heater</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Sizing to the Wall
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a wall-mount or recessed linear unit, the common rule of thumb is to choose a width that looks proportional to the wall and to the TV, if there is one above it. Many people match the fireplace width to the TV width or go slightly narrower. A 50-inch unit like the Touchstone Sideline suits a typical living room wall, while a 60-inch unit like the SimpliFire Allusion Platinum suits a larger feature wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Depth matters most for recessed installs. A unit around 5 to 6 inches deep, like the Allusion Platinum, can recess into many standard walls with modest framing, while deeper units need a built-out chase. Check the manufacturer's rough-opening dimensions, not just the product dimensions, because the opening usually has to be slightly larger and may need clearance at the top for the heater outlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a TV goes above the fireplace, check the manufacturer's minimum clearance between the heater outlet and the TV. Many linear units blow warm air out of the top or front, and a TV mounted too close can sit in that warm airflow. Some installers add a mantel or shelf between the two to deflect heat; follow the manual's figure rather than guessing.
          </p>

          <p>
            For built-in fireboxes, the firebox depth decides how far the surround projects into the room. The Dimplex Revillusion is listed at about 26.6 inches deep, which means a substantial surround. For media consoles, measure the TV's stand footprint and weight and compare them with the console's rating, and remember that the console's own width, 65 inches on the Dimplex Jesse, sets a practical limit on TV size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flame" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Flame Realism
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flame realism is the hardest thing to judge from a listing and the thing buyers care about most. Older and budget units project a flat, repeating pattern onto a back panel. Better units add depth with reflective panels, layered LEDs and realistic log sets, and some premium units use water vapor to create a three-dimensional smoke-like flame, as in Dimplex's Opti-myst cassettes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch videos of the exact model in a dimly lit room before you buy. Brightness, color and speed settings make a large difference, and many units default to a fast, bright orange flame that looks artificial. The ability to slow the flame, dim it and choose warmer colors is worth more than an extra few hundred BTU.
          </p>

          <p>
            Linear wall units tend to use colorful ember beds and media such as crystals or driftwood, which suit modern rooms but look less like a real fire. Built-in fireboxes with log sets, such as the Revillusion, are designed to resemble a traditional hearth. Decide which look you want before you compare prices, because it narrows the field quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-vapor units add realism but also maintenance: they have a reservoir to fill and, in some cases, a mineral cartridge to change, and their effect works best in a room without strong drafts. If you want a fireplace you never have to think about, an LED unit is the simpler choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Running Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running cost is simple to estimate. A 1,500-watt heater uses 1.5 kilowatt-hours for every hour it runs at full power. At an electricity rate of 17 cents per kilowatt-hour, which is a reasonable US average assumption, that is about 26 cents per hour. Run it at full heat for four hours a night and you spend about a dollar a night, or roughly $30 a month.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice the cost is usually lower, because the thermostat cycles the heater off once the room reaches the set temperature, and the low heat setting uses about half the power. The flame effect on its own uses very little electricity on most LED units, so running the flame without the heater in mild weather costs only a few cents an evening.
          </p>

          <p>
            A hardwired 240-volt unit putting out more heat uses proportionally more electricity. The Revillusion figure of about 2,575 watts works out to roughly 44 cents an hour at full output on the same 17-cent assumption. The extra heat is real, but so is the extra cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your goal is lower heating bills, an electric fireplace will not get you there on its own. Resistance heat costs more per unit of warmth than a modern heat pump, which can deliver several units of heat for each unit of electricity. Our guide to the <a href="/best-mini-split-heat-pump/">best mini-split heat pump</a> explains when that is the better investment. An electric fireplace earns its place as zone heat for the room you are in, plus the look of a fire.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Placement and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric fireplaces are among the safer ways to add a fire look to a home because there is no combustion, no flue and no open flame. They still contain a heater, so the general rules for space heaters apply: keep curtains, bedding, furniture and anything flammable away from the heater outlet, follow the manufacturer's clearances, and do not cover the unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for a model listed by a recognized testing laboratory, with overheat protection and, for freestanding units, a tip-over or cool-touch design. Most of the picks above list a thermostat and automatic shut-off. Our guide to the <a href="/best-electric-space-heater/">best electric space heater</a> covers these safety features in more detail, and they apply equally to fireplace heaters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not install a wall-mount unit in a bathroom or other wet location unless the manual specifically allows it, and keep children and pets away from the glass and heater outlet, which can get warm in operation. If you are recessing a unit into an exterior wall, check the manual for insulation and clearance requirements inside the cavity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, have a licensed electrician handle any hardwired installation, any new circuit and any recessed install where you are not confident about what is inside the wall. It is a modest cost against the price of the fireplace, and it is the difference between a heater that runs for years and one that trips breakers every evening.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting more heat from a bigger screen</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 60-inch plug-in fireplace usually has the same 1,500-watt heater as a 30-inch one. Width changes the look, not the heat. If heat matters, compare the electrical rating and look at hardwired 240-volt models rather than paying for extra inches of glass.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging into a shared or extension circuit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,500-watt heater draws about 12.5 amps, most of a 15-amp circuit. Sharing it with other loads trips breakers, and extension cords can overheat. Use a dedicated wall outlet, and have an electrician add one if the cord does not reach.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying before checking the rough opening</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Recessed and built-in units need a rough opening slightly larger than the product, plus clearances. Buying first and framing second leads to expensive rework. Download the installation manual and plan the opening before you order.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mounting the TV too close above</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many wall units push warm air out of the top. A TV mounted inside the manufacturer's minimum clearance sits in that airflow. Follow the manual's figure, and consider a mantel or shelf between the fireplace and the TV.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Believing whole-house heating claims</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,500-watt fireplace is supplemental zone heat. Claims of heating 1,000 square feet assume ideal conditions. For heating a whole floor efficiently, a heat pump or central system is the right tool.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much heat does an electric fireplace put out?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most plug-in electric fireplaces use a 1,500-watt heater, which is about 5,100 BTU, the same as a typical space heater. That is enough to warm a room of around 400 square feet as supplemental heat. Hardwired 240-volt models, such as some built-in fireboxes, can put out more; the Dimplex Revillusion is listed at up to about 8,800 BTU at 240 volts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do electric fireplaces need a dedicated circuit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many manufacturers recommend one. A 1,500-watt unit draws about 12.5 amps, most of a standard 15-amp circuit, so other loads on the same circuit can trip the breaker. Hardwired 240-volt models always need a dedicated circuit installed to the manual's specification, which is normally a job for a licensed electrician.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run an electric fireplace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At full heat a 1,500-watt unit uses 1.5 kWh per hour, about 26 cents an hour at 17 cents per kWh. The thermostat cycles the heater off once the room is warm, so real costs are usually lower. Running only the flame effect on an LED unit typically costs a few cents an evening.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a TV above an electric fireplace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes, if you follow the manufacturer's minimum clearance between the heater outlet and the TV. Many wall units vent warm air upward, so mounting the TV too close puts it in that airflow. A mantel or shelf between the two can help deflect heat. Check the manual for your exact model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric fireplaces safe to leave on overnight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are generally safer than combustion fireplaces because there is no flame or exhaust, but they still contain a heater. Use the thermostat and timer, keep flammable items clear of the outlet, and choose a model listed by a recognized testing lab with overheat protection. Follow the manual's guidance on unattended operation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I recess an electric fireplace into an existing wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many linear units are designed for it. The Touchstone Sideline 50, for example, is designed to recess into a 2x6 wall. You will need to cut drywall, frame the opening to the manufacturer's rough-opening dimensions and provide power. Exterior walls may have insulation and clearance requirements, so read the manual first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do electric fireplaces lower heating bills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if they let you turn down the central heat and warm just the room you are in. Electric resistance heat is more expensive per unit of warmth than a modern heat pump. An electric fireplace is best treated as zone heat plus ambience rather than a way to cut heating costs across the whole house.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

